<speak>
<prosody rate="85%" pitch="-2%" volume="+2dB">
<mark name="stage_pre_talk"/><p>You made it here today. <break time="400ms"/>You are sitting in that chair. <break time="400ms"/>You can hear the sound of my voice clearly. <break time="500ms"/>And that means the part of you that wants this change is already at work. <break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>So before we begin, here is what hypnosis actually is. <break time="500ms"/>
It is a natural state — you drift in and out of it every day, in movies, in daydreams, on familiar drives. <break time="600ms"/>
It is a narrow, comfortable focus that lets the protective filter of the mind relax, so a negative pattern can be exchanged for a positive one. <break time="700ms"/>
When I say the words deep sleep, that is a cue for this relaxed state — not actual sleep. <break time="500ms"/>
You stay aware the whole time, you hear everything, you cannot get stuck, and you would never do or say anything you wouldn't do wide awake. It is not a truth serum. <break time="800ms"/>If your mind wanders, that is normal — it will return to my voice on its own. <break time="600ms"/>
Some people find that they feel light and tingly, some feel pleasantly heavy, some simply feel calm. Whatever you experience is right for you. <break time="700ms"/>
And you may begin to wonder whether you are hypnotized — that wondering is usually the sign that you are.</p><p>This is a do-with process, not a do-to process — your participation and imagination are the engine. <break time="600ms"/>
The reason you are here is greater day-to-day comfort and ease alongside the care plan directed by Dr. Patel, and as you begin to work with the subconscious directly, the change you have been forcing becomes far easier. <break time="700ms"/>
I will run a few tests today to learn how your mind takes in information. You cannot pass or fail them — so no helping, no hindering — they simply tell me how to work in your exact style. <break time="600ms"/>
Any questions before we start? <mark name="await_questions"/><break time="4s"/>Good — and because of that, today we begin building the new associations that carry you to your goal.</p>
<mark name="stage_tom"/><p>Let me show you how the mind is organized, because it explains exactly why this works. <break time="600ms"/>
At the base sits the primitive area — pure survival instinct — with two settings: fight, which pushes through stress, and flight, the urge to withdraw and escape it. <break time="800ms"/>From birth to about age eight, the mind accepts nearly everything as true. That is when the programming forms — some of it positive, some negative — and those experiences become the known associations: what we believe, what feels familiar. <break time="900ms"/>
Those associations make up the subconscious mind — about eighty-eight percent of your mental power. <break time="700ms"/>
As we grow, a filter forms above it — the critical mind — whose whole job is to protect the known, even when the known is the problem. <break time="700ms"/>
And on top, the conscious mind: logic, reasoning, decisions, willpower — the willpower that never quite works when you need it — only about twelve percent of the power.</p><p>So here is what has been happening with greater day-to-day comfort and ease alongside the care plan directed by Dr. Patel. <break time="500ms"/>
The decision to change starts in the conscious mind, travels down, hits the critical filter, and gets checked against the old programming. <break time="600ms"/>
The programming resists, the filter blocks the change, and the same pattern runs again — the subconscious treats every signal as a fresh alarm, so attention and tension turn the volume up <break time="800ms"/>And you might notice how that pattern has been running on its own, without your permission. <break time="600ms"/>
Hypnosis takes that same intention and moves it past the filter, straight to the subconscious, where we install the new pattern with positive suggestion: in hypnosis we practice the comfort-dial and soft-body response so the nervous system rehearses ease, supporting the medical plan <break time="700ms"/>
I will work with the eighty-eight percent. You work with the twelve. Together, that is one hundred percent. <break time="600ms"/>That makes sense, doesn't it? <mark name="await_tom_yes"/><break time="4s"/>
And the way we begin is by testing your suggestibility.</p>
<mark name="stage_induction"/>
<p>Sit upright, feet flat on the floor, and rest your left arm on the table. <break time="600ms"/>
Look at that arm now, from the elbow to the fingertips — take a good picture of it — and close your eyes. <break time="900ms"/>
See that arm and hand in your mind, and when you can remember exactly what they look like, nod your head. <mark name="await_visualize_arm"/><break time="4s"/></p>
<mark name="stage_sugg_questions"/>
<p>Keep the eyes closed and just answer naturally. <break time="500ms"/>
Have you ever walked or talked in your sleep? <mark name="await_q1"/><break time="4s"/>
Have you ever woken in the night feeling unable to move or speak for a moment? <mark name="await_q2"/><break time="4s"/>
After an argument, do you replay what you could have said? <mark name="await_q3"/><break time="4s"/>
If you think of biting into a sour lemon, does your mouth water? <mark name="await_q4"/><break time="4s"/>
Can you picture things clearly in your mind? <mark name="await_q5"/><break time="4s"/></p>
<mark name="stage_physio"/>
<p>Now, as you begin to enter hypnosis, your body will show a series of changes. I will notice them before you do — when you become aware of each one, just nod and let me know. <break time="800ms"/>
First, the breathing tends to grow deeper as the body takes in more oxygen. When you notice your breathing deepening, nod your head. <mark name="await_breath"/><break time="4s"/>
The lips, mouth, and throat may feel a little dry, with an urge to swallow. Completely natural — when you notice it, nod. <mark name="await_swallow"/><break time="4s"/>
And notice the eyes now — that faint fluttering of the eyes beneath the lids. <break time="700ms"/>
That is the first stage of hypnosis.</p>
<mark name="stage_conversion"/>
<p>Let your attention drift to the elbow resting comfortably on the table, down through the forearm, into the wrist, the hand, the fingertips. <break time="700ms"/>And you might imagine that hand becoming lighter — as if many powerful helium balloons were tied around the wrist — light as a feather, and then even lighter. <break time="900ms"/>As you allow that, you may notice little pulling or tugging motions beginning — you are creating that sensation, and that gives you a greater sense of control. <break time="900ms"/>And at the same time you can feel the other hand a little heavier on your leg, the feet against the floor, the rhythm of your breath — and when you notice the light hand and the heavy hand feeling different, you can nod. <mark name="await_weight_diff"/><break time="4s"/>And it becomes a floating feeling — a tendency to <emphasis level="strong">lift with each breath</emphasis> — rising now, pivoting, the palm turning gently inward — and I wonder whether it feels more like the hand being drawn to the face, or the face to the hand, or both drawn together. I will let you decide. <break time="1200ms"/></p><p>And when the hand comes all the way in to touch the face — the moment you make skin contact — nod your head, and you will reach your peak of suggestibility and enter the hypnotic sleep. <mark name="await_skin_contact"/><break time="4s"/>
<mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1500ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p><mark name="stage_count_5_0"/>
<p>Gently let the hand come back down to rest. <break time="800ms"/>
Now a count from five down to zero, and each count carries you deeper. <break time="700ms"/>
Five — going down now, deeper into hypnosis. <break time="800ms"/>
Four — the body releasing, relaxing, letting go. <break time="800ms"/>
Three — an emotional calmness. <break time="800ms"/>
Two — the mind relaxing now. <break time="800ms"/>
One — deeper and deeper. <break time="800ms"/>
And zero — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_eye_fascination"/>
<p>Let the eyes come open for a moment, and pick a spot on the ceiling a little above your eye line — so the eyes look up just slightly to find it — and rest your gaze there. <break time="900ms"/>
Let the eyes settle on that one point, without moving them. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>As you keep looking at that spot — the gaze may drift and come back, gently, easily — you can begin to notice the small muscles around the eyes, <break time="400ms"/>how they hold the eyes up, <break time="400ms"/>and the eyelids, and what they feel like. <break time="900ms"/>
You may begin to blink a little more often <mark name="await_ef_blink"/><break time="4s"/>
That's it — and the breathing might deepen as you relax. <break time="800ms"/>
As the eyes rest on that spot, the mind wrapped around it, the eyelids may start to feel heavy, and the eyes may want to close. But not just yet — let them stay open a little longer. <break time="900ms"/>
And blinking a little more now <mark name="await_ef_blink2"/><break time="4s"/>
you might notice that blink is a little slower than normal. <break time="800ms"/>
Many people, entering hypnosis, find the focus going soft — the vision blurring — and as you look at that spot it might waver, or blur, or seem to shift shape or color. <break time="1000ms"/>That's it. <break time="700ms"/>
And you may notice the arms and legs growing heavy as the eyelids grow heavier and heavier, and how good it would feel to let those tired eyes simply relax and close. <break time="900ms"/>
As you focus on that spot and the eyes grow more tired, heavier, more fatigued — when you are ready, you can let them close. <mark name="await_ef_close"/><break time="4s"/>
And closing the eyes means you can relax now — it feels good, peaceful, as you really let yourself go. <break time="900ms"/></p><p>Breathe in for a moment <break time="700ms"/>and breathe out — and let that relaxation double through the whole body. <break time="900ms"/>
Deeper and deeper, all the way down. <break time="800ms"/>
Five — the body relaxing — four — the mind letting go — three — emotionally calm — two — one — zero — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="moderate">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_reactional"/>
<p>In a moment — not yet — I will ask you to open your eyes and fix them on a point above you, and when I say deep sleep, they close again and you go twice as deep. <break time="900ms"/>
Open your eyes. <break time="2s"/>And — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> Twice as deep. <break time="1200ms"/>
Open your eyes. <break time="2s"/>And — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1200ms"/>
Open your eyes. <break time="2s"/>And — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> Twice as deep. <break time="1200ms"/>
One more time — open your eyes. <break time="2s"/>And — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1500ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_deepener"/>
<p>Picture a level garden path with 20 smooth stepping stones, each one carrying you deeper into calm. <break time="800ms"/>
20 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>19 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>18 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>17 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>16 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>15 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>14 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>13 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>12 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>11 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>10 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>9 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>8 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>7 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>6 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>5 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>4 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>3 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>2 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>1 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>At the last stone you settle onto a comfortable bench, twice as deep.</p> <break time="1500ms"/>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_prog_relax"/><p>Rest back now, feeling the rhythm of your breath, giving yourself permission to let me guide you. <break time="900ms"/>Your breath is moving in its own rhythm, naturally and easily. <break time="600ms"/>
and you can feel the first wave of softening begin, <break time="500ms"/>You can see, in the mind's eye, a soft light of calm resting at the crown of the head, <break time="500ms"/>you can hear how quiet and even the breath is becoming. <break time="900ms"/>
Bring your attention to the top of the head, and let a wave of relaxation wash down over the scalp, <break time="400ms"/>the forehead, <break time="400ms"/>the temples, <break time="400ms"/>the eyelids and cheeks, loosening the jaw. <break time="800ms"/>Any outside sounds are only the everyday sounds of life — they cannot distract you; they simply send you deeper. <break time="900ms"/>The calm flows through the neck and shoulders, softening them, <break time="600ms"/>down the upper arms, the elbows, the forearms, releasing the wrists, the hands, all the way to the fingertips. <break time="900ms"/>
Into the chest now, opening, letting go of anything that no longer serves you, <break time="600ms"/>down through the center, the belly unwinding. <break time="800ms"/>The deeper you go, the more comfortable you become — the breathing deep, gentle, rhythmic — that drowsy, daydream feeling taking over — letting go, drifting down. <break time="900ms"/>
Through the hips, <break time="500ms"/>down the thighs, the large muscles and the small, <break time="500ms"/>the knees, the shins, the calves, <break time="600ms"/>the ankles, the heels, the arches, every toe loose to the very tips. <break time="1000ms"/>And now a count from five to zero, each count deeper still — five, letting go — four — three — two — one — zero — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_guided_imagery"/>
<p>To begin, simply focus. Feel your breath. <break time="600ms"/>
An easy breath in through the nose, and slowly out through the mouth. <break time="800ms"/>
And in your own mind, give yourself permission to allow me to guide you on a pleasant journey. <break time="700ms"/>
On the exhale of your third or fourth breath, let your eyes close. <break time="1500ms"/>
Although not everyone visualizes, everyone can imagine — if you can't see it as a picture, that's alright; we'll use the other senses too, or simply think about it, or conceptualize. <break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Let's start at the top of the head, and gently work downward, allowing any pressure inside to release. <break time="600ms"/>
Down over the face, especially the jaw, releasing tension — a pleasant feeling you're allowing. <break time="600ms"/>
Flowing down over the shoulders, down the arms and hands, the pressures of the day releasing. <break time="600ms"/>
Down through the chest and the back, the stomach and lower back, <break time="500ms"/>through the hips, and all the way down the legs to the tips of the toes — a gentle letting go. <break time="900ms"/>
Now count backward in your own mind with me, from five down to zero — five, <break time="500ms"/>four, <break time="500ms"/>three, even deeper, <break time="500ms"/>two, <break time="500ms"/>one, <break time="500ms"/>and zero — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="moderate">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>Feel the rhythm of your breath, and let your imaginative mind open. <break time="700ms"/>
Imagine we're walking through a beautiful meadow on a wonderful day, the temperature just as you'd like it. <break time="700ms"/>
You feel the earth beneath your feet, a sweet smell of nature around you, perhaps wildflowers in bloom. <break time="700ms"/>
A beautiful bird crosses the sky — majestic and free — and a free feeling rises in you. <break time="700ms"/>
You hear a gentle breeze through distant trees. <break time="700ms"/>
Ahead, on a little hill beyond the meadow, stands a single, beautiful tree. <break time="600ms"/>
As you come closer you see how wonderfully it's formed, leaves moving in the breeze — the sounds and smells and images all easing you. <break time="800ms"/>
Near the top, on the tip of one branch, a single leaf. <break time="600ms"/>
You stop and watch it twist and turn, twist and turn — and something in you knows it's about to break free. <break time="700ms"/>
And all at once the leaf is free, the breeze carrying it slowly, gliding down, down toward the earth, to rest near the base of the tree. <break time="800ms"/>
You walk over and look down — the earth, the leaf, the roots of the tree — and there, a single blade of grass. <break time="700ms"/>
It has persevered and grown strong through its own motivation — the same way you are motivated in your life. <break time="800ms"/>
And you can take what we've talked about, begin to understand it, and help yourself with it — and that feels wonderful. <break time="900ms"/>
So in this beautiful place — the smells, the sounds, the positive feelings in body and mind — we carry all of it forward with you. <break time="1000ms"/></p><mark name="stage_suggestions"/>
<p>And in this deep, receptive state, the part of you that knows how to change is listening now. <break time="900ms"/>
Your subconscious accepts what serves you, naturally and easily. <break time="800ms"/>
You might imagine comfort as a warmth that spreads a little further each time you rest like this. <break time="1100ms"/>
Perhaps the sensation can move to arm's length for a while — noticed, respected, and quieter. <break time="1100ms"/>
And you may find the body remembering how to rest around a signal instead of gripping it. <break time="1100ms"/>
It can be reassuring to know your medical team leads the way, while this skill of ease travels with you. <break time="1100ms"/>
</p><p>Now see it. <break time="500ms"/>
Picture a calm room where everyone is at ease — and within that scene, watch yourself living the change — the body unlearns bracing; attention widens; the signal is acknowledged and then turned down like a dial <break time="1200ms"/>
Like steady hands that steady others. <break time="900ms"/>And feel it — <break time="400ms"/>let the body register how this new pattern feels: a deep, private steadiness, and the quiet certainty that this change lasts. <break time="1200ms"/>There is no rush — it is unfolding now, naturally and easily, with each breath. <break time="800ms"/>And whether you notice the first change tonight, or sometime tomorrow, or in a quiet moment you did not expect — the change is already underway, and you can <emphasis level="moderate">trust yourself now</emphasis>. <break time="900ms"/>You are not becoming someone new. You already know how to do this. You are remembering who you have always been. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>In sleep, the body can continue this softening on its own, so rest becomes deeper and mornings easier. <break time="900ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_emerge"/><p>It is time now to come back up, calm and refreshed. I will count from zero to five, and with each number you grow more alert, more awake, with a positive sense of well-being. <break time="900ms"/>Zero — we close the door to the subconscious, sealing out every negative. <break time="700ms"/>
One — slowly and calmly coming up. <break time="700ms"/>
Two — physically aware, mentally alert. <break time="700ms"/>
Three — coming up higher now, and you may begin to wonder how good you feel. <break time="700ms"/>
Four — the eyes beginning to open, and — <break time="600ms"/>
Five — <emphasis level="strong">eyes open, wide awake.</emphasis> <break time="600ms"/>
One two three four five — eyes open, wide awake.</p>
<mark name="stage_finger_spread"/>
<p>Before we finish, one quick check. Sit with your feet flat and rest your hands on your legs. <break time="600ms"/>
Raise one arm, bend it at the elbow, and hold the palm about ten inches in front of your face, fingers straight, just above eye level. <break time="900ms"/>
Stare at the middle finger. Take a deep breath — and on the exhale, the fingers begin to separate. <break time="900ms"/>
Separating — wider and wider, further and further apart. <break time="900ms"/>
Separating — wider and wider, further and further apart. <break time="900ms"/>
Separating — wider and wider, further and further apart. <break time="1000ms"/>
And as the fingers keep separating — tell me, is it the hand, or the arm, that is starting to pull in toward your face? <mark name="await_hand_or_arm"/><break time="4s"/>
Good — it keeps pulling in closer and closer as the fingers separate — and when you notice your breathing getting deeper, taking in more oxygen, nod and let me know. <mark name="await_fs_breath"/><break time="4s"/>
When the eyelids start to blink and grow heavy, nod — and the more they blink, the heavier they become, and the heavier they become, the more they blink — so heavy now they would much rather stay closed. <mark name="await_fs_eyes"/><break time="4s"/>
Closer and closer with every breath — and when the hand comes all the way in to touch the face, nod — the moment you make skin contact, the eyes close, and you reach your peak and enter the hypnotic sleep. <mark name="await_fs_contact"/><break time="4s"/>
<mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1500ms"/>
Perfect. <break time="500ms"/>And now — one two three four five — eyes open, wide awake.</p>
<mark name="stage_homework"/><p>Before next session: <break time="400ms"/>
Twice daily, five minutes: self-guided comfort-dial practice in the chosen scene. <break time="600ms"/>
Keep the comfort log (0–10, morning and evening) and bring it — the log is shared with Dr. Patel on request. <break time="600ms"/>
Picture next week for a moment — you, walking in, telling me the first win. <break time="600ms"/>Same time next week, and notice what changes in the meantime. Sooner or later, you will be surprised by how naturally it unfolds.</p>
</prosody>
</speak>